  "text": "**Solution 2 is wrong.** Its path analysis is correct: on a path, each interior vertex has two neighbours, so the update is the midpoint for every p, and β_p ≥ 3 for all p. But it then assumes no other graph is slower, and that assumption fails for p < 2. Its own table omits the complete graph. There, with boundary values 0 and 1 and h ≡ 1/2, near-equilibrium neighbour differences are of order ε, and the weights |s|^{p-2} blow up for p < 2, so updates barely move the interior toward h. The problem's split between a log bound for p ≥ 2 and a polynomial ε^{-c(p)} bound for p < 2 points the same way. Also, a constant answer of 3 conflicts with the question's request for \"an explicit function of p.\"\n\n**Solution 1 is more plausible, though not verified.** Its lower bounds are heuristic. The complete-graph mean-field ODE gives τ ~ n^{p/(p-1)} ε^{-(2-p)/(p-1)}, which is a reasonable scaling for p < 2. The path bound of n³ is correct and p-independent. Combining them gives max(3, p/(p-1)), which is 3 at p = 2, consistent with the known linear case. Its matching upper bound for p < 3/2 is asserted rather than proved, and the problem's choice of the cutoff p < 3 suggests the true formula may differ from this one. So I rate Solution 1 as the better candidate, not as confirmed.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
